steps.say:
An array variable does not hold the numbers themselves. It holds a reference: directions to where the array lives.
static void drain(int[] a) { a[0] = 0; }
int[] potions = {5, 5, 5};
drain(potions);
The call copies the reference into the parameter a. Now a and potions are two slips with the same directions. There is still only one array.

beats.code:
static void drain(int[] a) {
    a[0] = 0;
}

int[] potions = {5, 5, 5};
drain(potions);

steps.say:
Reassigning the parameter is different:
a = new int[]{9, 9, 9};
That rewrites only the method's own slip, so a now points at a brand-new array. The caller's potions still points at the old one and sees nothing. The pattern: changing an ELEMENT reaches the caller; pointing the PARAMETER somewhere else does not.

