steps.say:
Say a method must report both the gold and the gems found in a chest. I bundle them into one object, and a record is the shortest way to describe such a bundle:
record Loot(int gold, int gems) {}
That single line declares a new type named Loot with two parts, gold and gems.

Now Loot can be a return type:
static Loot open(int level) { return new Loot(level * 10, level * 2); }
new Loot(...) builds one bundle from two values, in the order the record lists them, and return hands the whole bundle back.

beats.code:
static Loot open(int level) {
    return new Loot(level * 10, level * 2);
}

steps.say:
The caller stores the bundle, then reads each part by calling an accessor named after it, parentheses included:
Loot found = open(3);
System.out.println(found.gold());
That prints 30.

